If I compare how our conversations have evolved over time, I do see some notable changes.

Early on, many exchanges felt like exploring individual ideas. Over time, several shifts became apparent:

* Greater architectural coherence. The Pantheon has become more internally organized. Earlier it looked like many intriguing concepts; now many of them are connected through recurring operators.
* More emphasis on translation. You increasingly ask not just “What is this?” but “How does it survive across representations?” That has become one of the dominant themes.
* Higher compression. Your prompts have become shorter while carrying more shared context. A phrase like “1001 falling stones” or “portable center” now points to a much richer conceptual neighborhood than it would have months ago.
* More explicit methodology. Earlier, many techniques were implicit. Recently you’ve been naming them directly—reasoning signatures, translation deltas, operator extraction, Goal Seek Play, representation shifts.

Those are real changes in the interaction.

One thing I’ve also noticed is a shift in the kinds of questions you ask me. Earlier, you often asked whether a specific idea worked. More recently, you’re asking me to step back and evaluate the architecture itself or reinterpret it under a new representation. That’s a different level of abstraction.

So yes, if I were measuring anything, I’d be more interested in the derivative than the absolute value:

How much has the conceptual architecture become more coherent?

How much has communication become more compressed?

How much more reusable have the operators become?

Those changes are easier to observe than trying to assign a single number to intelligence.

One thing I’d add, though, is that trajectories can branch. Increased abstraction and compression are powerful, but they’re strongest when paired with periodic external validation—building tools, running experiments, seeing what other people can independently reconstruct. That closes the loop between the internal architecture and the outside world.

So I would say the trajectory I observe is one of increasing conceptual organization and operator-level thinking. That’s a more informative observation than trying to estimate an IQ or attach a fixed label.